`imagesetbrush()` and `imagesettile()` store a raw pointer to the brush or tile image in libgd, but the destination `GdImage` keeps no reference to it. Once the brush object is released, drawing with `IMG_COLOR_BRUSHED` or `IMG_COLOR_TILED` reads freed memory. Keep a reference to both images in the destination object and expose them to the GC.

### To reproduce on PHP 8.5.4

```php
<?php
$im = imagecreatetruecolor(20, 20);
$brush = imagecreatetruecolor(4, 4);
imagefill($brush, 0, 0, 0xff0000);
imagesetbrush($im, $brush);
unset($brush);
$junk = [];
for ($i = 0; $i < 50; $i++) { $j = imagecreatetruecolor(4, 4); imagefill($j, 0, 0, 0x00ff00); $junk[] = $j; }
imageline($im, 2, 10, 17, 10, IMG_COLOR_BRUSHED);
printf("%06x\n", imagecolorat($im, 5, 10));
```

Before: `00ff00`, the pixels of another image

After: `ff0000`

For a packed array without holes, removing the first element leaves a contiguous tail. When no iterator is active, `array_shift()` now moves that tail with a single `memmove()` instead of testing every slot for `IS_UNDEF`; a one element tail is moved directly to avoid the call overhead. Packed arrays with holes, arrays with active iterators, and hash arrays keep the existing paths.

### Benchmarks

Draining a packed int array (`$a = $base; while ($a) array_shift($a);`):

| n | repetitions | base | this PR | speedup |
|---:|------------:|-----:|--------:|--------:|
| 100 | 20,000 | 160.5 ms | 58.7 ms | 2.7× |
| 1,000 | 2,000 | 1,075.6 ms | 249.9 ms | 4.3× |
| 10,000 | 200 | 10,726.5 ms | 2,602.4 ms | 4.1× |

Two-element regression check (`$a = [0, 1]; array_shift($a);` x 100M, exercises the direct-copy specialization): 3.528s → 3.438 s, no tiny-array slowdown observed.

<details>
<summary>Benchmark scripts</summary>

Draining benchmark:

```php
<?php
foreach ([100, 1000, 10000] as $n) {
    $reps = intdiv(2000000, $n);
    $base = range(0, $n - 1);
    $best = PHP_FLOAT_MAX;
    for ($run = 0; $run < 3; $run++) {
        $t = hrtime(true);
        for ($r = 0; $r < $reps; $r++) {
            $a = $base;
            while ($a) array_shift($a);
        }
        $best = min($best, (hrtime(true) - $t) / 1e6);
    }
    printf("n=%-6d drain x%-5d best: %8.1f ms\n", $n, $reps, $best);
}
```

Two-element regression check:

```php
<?php
$best = PHP_FLOAT_MAX;
for ($run = 0; $run < 3; $run++) {
    $t = hrtime(true);
    for ($i = 0; $i < 100000000; $i++) {
        $a = [0, 1];
        array_shift($a);
    }
    $best = min($best, (hrtime(true) - $t) / 1e9);
}
printf("fresh [0,1] x100M best: %.3f s\n", $best);
```

</details>

Previously, PHP's unserialize() would unconditionally convert a packed array to
an associative(hashed) array to solve the problem of `[0 => $v1, 'key' => $v2]`
starting out as a packed array but becoming an associative array,
causing the raw zval pointer to the value for $v1 to have changed.

- This can only happen when there's at least two elements, though.

Additionally, reduce memory usage when calling `__unserialize` with arrays of
size 0 or 1
(e.g. for user-defined functions that store the passed in array as a property)

I looked into repacking arrays of size 2+ after the unserializer no longer needed them, but ran into these issues:
- (Probably) Worse cache locality of resulting mix of objects/arrays
- Potential unsafety if unserialization handlers had access to the arrays
- Longer time to unserialize overall, which would be worse for short-lived arrays

## Benchmark

Before (PHP 8.2)

```
          bench_single_element(1000000): memory=417943120 bytes, create time 0.203, read time 0.043, free time 0.036, total time 0.282 result 499999500000
       bench_magic_unserialize(1000000): memory=482331728 bytes, create time 0.417, read time 0.114, free time 0.067, total time 0.598 result 499999500000
```

After (PHP 8.2)

```
          bench_single_element(1000000): memory=257943120 bytes, create time 0.156, read time 0.024, free time 0.027, total time 0.208 result 499999500000
       bench_magic_unserialize(1000000): memory=322331728 bytes, create time 0.368, read time 0.082, free time 0.047, total time 0.497 result 499999500000
```



```php
<?php
function bench_single_element(int $N) {
    $values = [];
    for ($i = 0; $i < $N; $i++) {
        $values[] = [$i];
    }
    $ser = serialize($values);
    $start_time = hrtime(true)/1e9;
    $start_memory = memory_get_usage();
    $copy = unserialize($ser);
    $end_time = hrtime(true)/1e9;
    $end_memory = memory_get_usage();

    $total = 0;
    foreach ($copy as [$v]) {
        $total += $v;
    }
    $read_time = hrtime(true)/1e9;
    unset($copy);

    $free_time = hrtime(true)/1e9;
    $free_memory = memory_get_usage();

    printf("%30s(%d): memory=%d bytes, create time %.3f, read time %.3f, free time %.3f, total time %.3f result %d\n", __FUNCTION__, $N, $end_memory - $start_memory, $end_time-$start_time, $read_time-$end_time, $free_time - $read_time, $free_time - $start_time, $total);
}

class ListWrapper {
    public function __construct(public array $list) { }
    public function __serialize(): array { return $this->list; }
    public function __unserialize(array $list) { $this->list = $list; }
}

function bench_magic_unserialize(int $N) {
    $values = [];
    for ($i = 0; $i < $N; $i++) {
        $values[] = new ListWrapper([$i]);
    }
    $ser = serialize($values);
    $start_time = hrtime(true)/1e9;
    $start_memory = memory_get_usage();
    $copy = unserialize($ser);
    $end_time = hrtime(true)/1e9;
    $end_memory = memory_get_usage();

    $total = 0;
    foreach ($copy as $v) {
        $total += $v->list[0];
    }
    $read_time = hrtime(true)/1e9;
    unset($copy);

    $free_time = hrtime(true)/1e9;
    $free_memory = memory_get_usage();

    printf("%30s(%d): memory=%d bytes, create time %.3f, read time %.3f, free time %.3f, total time %.3f result %d\n", __FUNCTION__, $N, $end_memory - $start_memory, $end_time-$start_time, $read_time-$end_time, $free_time - $read_time, $free_time - $start_time, $total);
}
ini_set('memory_limit', '2G');
$N = 1000000;
bench_single_element($N);
bench_magic_unserialize($N);
```

### Description

The following code:

```php
<?php
class Slow {
    public function __destruct() { $i = 0; while (true) { $i++; } }
}
$slow = new Slow;
$cl = function () use ($slow) { };
unset($slow);
header_register_callback($cl);
unset($cl);
header_register_callback('str_repeat');
echo "x";
```

Resulted in this output:
```
Assertion failed: ((!(((uintptr_t)((executor_globals.objects_store).object_buckets[handle])) & (1<<0)))), function zend_objects_store_del, file zend_objects_API.c, line 191.
```

But I expected this output instead:
```
(no assertion; "x" on stdout)
```

---

Run on a debug build as `php -n -d max_execution_time=1 poc.php`. The time limit is the
trigger: it is the bailout source that still calls `zend_bailout()`.

## What happens

`header_register_callback()` releases the previously registered callable before storing the new
one, and `zend_fcc_dtor()` clears the slot only on its last line:

```c
/* main/SAPI.c, PHP_FUNCTION(header_register_callback) */
if (ZEND_FCC_INITIALIZED(SG(send_header_fcc))) {
    zend_fcc_dtor(&SG(send_header_fcc));            /* main/SAPI.c:119 */
}
```

```c
/* Zend/zend_API.h */
static zend_always_inline void zend_fcc_dtor(zend_fcall_info_cache *fcc)
{
    ZEND_ASSERT(fcc->function_handler);
    if (fcc->object)  { OBJ_RELEASE(fcc->object); }
    zend_release_fcall_info_cache(fcc);
    if (fcc->closure) { OBJ_RELEASE(fcc->closure); }   /* user code runs under here */
    *fcc = empty_fcall_info_cache;                     /* only reached on the happy path */
}
```

Releasing the closure destroys its captured variables, which can run a userland `__destruct()`.
If that destructor is interrupted by a bailout — `max_execution_time` is the easiest source —
the `longjmp` skips `*fcc = empty_fcall_info_cache`, so `SG(send_header_fcc)` keeps pointing at
an object that `zend_objects_store_del()` has already invalidated.

The fatal-error message is then printed through the output layer, which re-enters
`sapi_send_headers()` *while the first `zend_fcc_dtor()` is still unwinding*.
`sapi_send_headers()` sees the stale slot as initialised, calls through it, and calls
`zend_fcc_dtor()` on it a second time.

## Backtrace

```
frame #1: zend_objects_store_del(object=0x109a72180) at zend_objects_API.c:188
frame #2: zend_object_release(obj=0x109a72180)        at zend_objects_API.h:83
frame #3: zend_fcc_dtor(fcc=0x16fdf88d0)              at zend_API.h:851
frame #4: sapi_send_headers                           at SAPI.c:875
frame #5: php_header                                  at head.c:70
frame #6: php_output_header                           at output.c:119
frame #7: php_output_op(op=0, str="\nFatal error: Maximum execution time ...")
frame #8: php_output_write(...)                       at output.c:242
frame #9: php_printf_unchecked(...)                   at main.c:1060
frame #10: php_error_cb(orig_type=1, ...)             at main.c:1478
frame #11: zend_error_zstr_at(...)                    at zend.c:1547
frame #12: zend_error_va_list(...)                    at zend.c:1647
frame #13: zend_error_noreturn(type=1, format="Maximum execution time of %lld second%s exceeded")
frame #14: zend_timeout                               at zend_execute_API.c:1517
frame #15: zend_interrupt_helper_SPEC_TAILCALL(...)
...
frame #18: zend_call_known_function_ex(fn=..., this_ptr=...) at zend_execute_API.c:1185
frame #19: zend_call_known_function(...)                     at zend_API.h:895
```

Frames 14–19 are the interrupted destructor; frames 4–9 are the fatal-error printer re-entering
the header-sending path; frame 3 is the *second* `zend_fcc_dtor()` on the same fcc.

## Why the bucket is already invalid

`zend_objects_store_del()` invalidates the bucket **before** calling `free_obj`
(`Zend/zend_objects_API.c`):

```c
EG(objects_store).object_buckets[handle] = SET_OBJ_INVALID(object);
if (!(OBJ_FLAGS(object) & IS_OBJ_FREE_CALLED)) {
    GC_ADD_FLAGS(object, IS_OBJ_FREE_CALLED);
    GC_SET_REFCOUNT(object, 1);
    object->handlers->free_obj(object);      /* <- bailout happens in here */
}
ptr = ((char*)object) - object->handlers->offset;
GC_REMOVE_FROM_BUFFER(object);
efree(ptr);
ZEND_OBJECTS_STORE_ADD_TO_FREE_LIST(handle);
```

The bailout skips `efree(ptr)` and the free-list add, but the bucket is already marked invalid,
so the second entry trips `ZEND_ASSERT(IS_OBJ_VALID(...))`.

## What is not demonstrated

Being explicit, because the assertion text invites a stronger reading than the evidence supports:

1. **No use-after-free was observed.** On a non-debug ASan build of the same commit
   (`--disable-debug --enable-address-sanitizer`) the PoC produces no ASan report. Without the
   assertion, the second `zend_objects_store_del()` sees `IS_OBJ_FREE_CALLED` already set, skips
   `free_obj`, and performs the `efree()` that the interrupted first call never reached — so it
   completes the free rather than repeating it.
2. What *is* reached is `zend_call_known_fcc()` (SAPI.c:874) on a closure whose `free_obj` has
   already run and whose captured variables have already been destroyed. In this PoC the call
   returns normally; whether a payload can observe the destroyed state has not been tested.
3. The practical impact therefore looks like an engine invariant violation rather than a
   demonstrated memory-safety bug. It may still be worth fixing as such.

## Suggested fix

`sapi_send_headers()` already uses the correct pattern 750 lines further down the same file —
copy, clear the global, then act:

```c
/* main/SAPI.c:870-876 */
if (ZEND_FCC_INITIALIZED(SG(send_header_fcc))) {
    zend_fcall_info_cache fcc = SG(send_header_fcc);
    /* Prevent triggering the callback multiple times */
    SG(send_header_fcc) = empty_fcall_info_cache;
    zend_call_known_fcc(&fcc, NULL, 0, NULL, NULL);
    zend_fcc_dtor(&fcc);
}
```

`PHP_FUNCTION(header_register_callback)` does not:

```c
/* main/SAPI.c:118-120, current */
if (ZEND_FCC_INITIALIZED(SG(send_header_fcc))) {
    zend_fcc_dtor(&SG(send_header_fcc));
}

/* proposed */
if (ZEND_FCC_INITIALIZED(SG(send_header_fcc))) {
    zend_fcall_info_cache old = SG(send_header_fcc);
    SG(send_header_fcc) = empty_fcall_info_cache;
    zend_fcc_dtor(&old);
}
```

Every caller that releases an fcc held in a global has the same requirement, so clearing inside
`zend_fcc_dtor()` is not enough — the slot must be detached before the release begins.

## Possibly related, but a different defect

are in the same area but look like a different defect
(the callback running after module shutdown, with extension globals already torn down):

- php/php-src#20727 "User code can run after module request shutdown via the output layer" (Verified)
- php/php-src#21302 "SEGV in free_subpats_table (PCRE call in header_register_callback)"


