This PR speeds up `array_splice()` when inserting without removing elements by allocating enough space upfront, avoiding repeated growth. Behavior is unchanged.

Inserting one element in the middle of an array on Apple M1 (release build), using the median of seven runs per build:

| input | base | this PR | speedup |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| packed, n=16 | 99.8 ns | 77.7 ns | 1.28× |
| packed, n=100,000 | 421 µs | 401 µs | within noise¹ |
| string keys, n=1,000 | 7.70 µs | 5.82 µs | 1.32× |
| string keys, n=100,000 | 1.74 ms | 1.05 ms | 1.66× |

¹ Across the seven runs, base ranged 413–427 µs and this PR 391–429 µs.

Inserting one element into an array with 100,000 string keys drops extra peak memory from 7.5 MiB to 5.0 MiB.

<details>
<summary>Benchmark scripts and methodology</summary>

Builds were benchmarked alternately. Each script run reports the lowest average time per iteration across five batches, including the `array_pop()`/`unset()` that keeps the array size constant.

Timing:

```php
<?php
function bench(string $label, array $a, int $reps): void {
    $packed = array_is_list($a);
    $mid = intdiv(count($a), 2);
    $best = PHP_FLOAT_MAX;

    for ($run = 0; $run < 5; $run++) {
        $t = hrtime(true);
        for ($r = 0; $r < $reps; $r++) {
            array_splice($a, $mid, 0, [-1]);
            if ($packed) {
                array_pop($a);
            } else {
                unset($a[0]);
            }
        }
        $best = min($best, (hrtime(true) - $t) / $reps);
    }

    printf("%-24s %10.1f ns/op\n", $label, $best);
}

$keys = fn(int $n) => array_combine(
    array_map(fn($i) => "k$i", range(0, $n - 1)),
    range(0, $n - 1)
);

bench('packed n=16', range(0, 15), 1000000);
bench('packed n=100,000', range(0, 99999), 1000);
bench('string keys n=1,000', $keys(1000), 50000);
bench('string keys n=100,000', $keys(100000), 300);
```

Extra peak memory, measured relative to usage before the call:

```php
<?php
$a = array_combine(
    array_map(fn($i) => "k$i", range(0, 99999)),
    range(0, 99999)
);

$before = memory_get_usage();
memory_reset_peak_usage();
array_splice($a, 50000, 0, [-1]);

printf(
    "extra peak: %.1f MiB\n",
    (memory_get_peak_usage() - $before) / 1048576
);
